# Verification log

Append only. One block per fetch attempt, per source. Never edit a past block.
Links rot, and the point of this file is to know when.

Format:

    key      the sources.json key
    url      what was requested
    date     when it was attempted, ISO 8601
    result   ok, redirected, dead, paywalled, egress_denied
    status   the HTTP status, or the transport error
    cached   the file under library/pdf/, or none
    contents one line on what the document actually holds, which is often not
             what its title suggests. Written by a person or a model that has
             read it. A fetch alone never fills this in.

================================================================================
SESSION ZERO, 2026-08-25. NOTHING WAS FETCHED.
================================================================================

All 40 attempts below failed for the same reason, and it is not link rot. The
environment this session ran in denies outbound network egress. Every host was
refused at the proxy before any request was sent.

Evidence, 2026-08-25:

  curl, WebFetch and pip were each tried. All returned the same class of
  failure. The agent proxy status endpoint reported, for every host:

      kind:   connect_rejected
      detail: gateway answered 403 to CONNECT (policy denial or upstream
              failure)

  Hosts confirmed refused by direct test:

      arxiv.org                        cds.cern.ch
      www.bnl.gov                      www.astm.org
      www.femm.info                    magpylib.readthedocs.io
      www.arnoldmagnetics.com          eprintspublications.npl.co.uk
      github.com                       pypi.org

  The syllabus cites 40 sources across 30 distinct hosts. None is reachable.
  PyPI being refused also means magpylib cannot be installed, so no worked.py
  can run here either.

What this means for the entries below. Every one records result egress_denied,
not dead. Do not treat any of them as a broken link. The URLs have not been
tested. When the network is open, run:

    python3 magnet-course/library/fetch_sources.py

It appends a fresh dated block for every source with the real HTTP status, and
updates the verification object in sources.json. Read what lands, then write
the contents line for each. Until then, no source in this course has been
verified and nothing may be cited.

